break — exit the innermost loop.
continue — skip to next iteration.
else after for/while — runs only if the loop finished without break.
for n in range(2, 20):
for d in range(2, n):
if n % d == 0:
print(f"{n} = {d} * {n // d}")
break
else:
print(f"{n} is prime")
The else runs only when the inner for completes without finding a divisor → the number is prime. This is a Python-unique idiom worth memorising.

The legendary interview problem. Print numbers 1–100. For multiples of 3, print "Fizz". For multiples of 5, print "Buzz". For multiples of both, print "FizzBuzz".
Solution
for i in range(1, 101):
if i % 15 == 0:
print("FizzBuzz")
elif i % 3 == 0:
print("Fizz")
elif i % 5 == 0:
print("Buzz")
else:
print(i)

Keep asking for numbers until the user enters an empty line. Print the count and average.
Solution
nums = []
while True:
s = input("Number (empty to stop): ")
if s == "":
break
nums.append(float(s))
if nums:
print(f"Count: {len(nums)}, average: {sum(nums) / len(nums):.2f}")
else:
print("No numbers entered.")
